\title{Introduction to set theory}
\date{2026-02-16T20:48:36Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{The theory of sets is of both practical and historic significance. It is of practical significance because working with collections of values is commonplace in computing and mathematics.  It is also of historical significance as one of the first attempts to put mathematics as a whole on a rigorous logical foundation.  Even today, if you ask most mathematicians what the foundation of mathematics is, they will give set theory as the answer.  On this view, set theory is sort of like the "assembly language" of mathematics: everything is ultimately "compiled down" into the language of set theory, even though no one thinks about it on a day-to-day basis. I would argue that something called \em{type theory} provides a much better foundation for mathematics as it is actually carried out—but that is a topic for a different course!}

